### Part 3 — Getting SYSTEM via PsExec (Sysinternals)

#### 3.1 Download PSTools

- Downloaded Chrome installer (ChromeSetup.exe) to get a browser working in the VM
- Searched Google for "PsExec Sysinternals" → downloaded PSTools.zip from Microsoft Learn
- Extracted to `C:\Users\Administrator\Downloads\PSTools\` → contains `PsExec.exe`, `PsExec64.exe`, `psfile.exe`, `PsGetsid.exe`, `PsInfo.exe`, etc.

#### 3.2 Get SYSTEM shell with PsExec

Open **Command Prompt** (as Administrator) and run:

```powershell
C:\Users\Administrator\Downloads\PSTools\PsExec64.exe -i -s powershell.exe
```

- `-s` → Run the remote process in the **System account**
- `-i` → Run the program so that it interacts with the desktop of the specified session
- `-accepteula` → Suppress license dialog

Verify with `whoami` → shows `nt authority\system`.

#### 3.3 (Alternative) PsExec to spawn PowerShell in System context

```powershell
psexec -i -s powershell.exe
```

